    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115230",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2\\le -4.$",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2 & \\le -4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -6 \\\\ (0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.72)^{3x-7}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.72)^{3x-7}\\le -12$ has no solutions.",
