    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115250",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality ${5}^{x-1}<{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-1} & <{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}] \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
