  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-365323",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality ${4}^{1-2x}\\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{1-2x} & \\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ (1-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
