    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-89661",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6{x}^{5}+18x>3x.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all terms to the left-hand side and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}+18x & >3x \\\\ 6{x}^{5}+15x & >0 \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{4}+5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2{x}^{4}+5$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{4}+5)}{2{x}^{4}+5} & >\\frac{0}{2{x}^{4}+5} \\\\ 3x & >0 \\\\ x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
