      "knowledge_point": "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)<0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $-3{x}^{2}-4$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)}{-3{x}^{2}-4} & >\\frac{0}{-3{x}^{2}-4} \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
