  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-73455",
      "knowledge_point_id": "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/de39663db59e42565c7d69d8a44a4cb95f14af0f4946b77cfc42eac02f7d2786.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$\n- $[-\\pi ,0)$\n- $[1,\\pi ]$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is closed,but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-\\pi ,0)$ is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
